"# Reef.js in brief\n\nNodus is built for [Reef.js](https://reefjs.com). This page explains what Reef is, where it comes\nfrom, and how it differs from the best-known JavaScript frameworks — enough to understand Nodus's\nchoices before writing a line.\n\n## What Reef is\n\nReef is a small library for building **reactive, state-driven** interfaces. Its API fits in five\nfunctions:\n\n| Function | Role |\n|---|---|\n| `signal(data)` | Makes an object or array reactive: every change emits a `reef:signal` event |\n| `component(elem, template)` | Binds an element to a template; re-renders it when a signal changes |\n| `render(elem, html)` | Updates an element once, without reactivity |\n| `store(data, setters)` | A signal whose changes go through named functions |\n| `focus(selector)` | Moves focus after the next render |\n\nA template is **a function that returns an HTML string**. Reef compares that HTML with the real DOM\nand only changes what differs. Version 13, used by Nodus, weighs about 5 KB minified (about 2 KB\ncompressed) and has no dependencies.\n\n```js\nimport { signal, component } from 'reefjs';\n\nconst state = signal({ count: 0 });\ncomponent('#app', () => `<p>${state.count} click(s)</p><button data-action=\"inc\">+1</button>`);\n\ndocument.addEventListener('click', e => {\n  if (e.target.closest('[data-action=\"inc\"]')) state.count++;\n});\n```\n\n## Who created it\n\nReef is written and maintained by **Chris Ferdinandi** (Go Make Things), author of many guides on\n“vanilla” JavaScript. It is released under the MIT license; Nodus credits it in `THIRD-PARTY.md`.\n\n## How it differs from React, Vue, Svelte or Angular\n\n| | Reef | React, Vue, Svelte, Angular |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Build** | None: ES modules in the browser | Compilation required or strongly advised (JSX, `.vue` / `.svelte` files, TypeScript and decorators) |\n| **Templates** | Strings (template literals) | JSX, a dedicated template syntax, or a compiled language |\n| **DOM updates** | Diffed against the real DOM, batched on an animation frame | Virtual DOM (React, Vue) or code generated at compile time (Svelte, Angular) |\n| **State** | Plain objects made reactive by `signal()` | Hooks, refs, stores, services and dependency injection |\n| **Events** | Native delegation (`addEventListener`); `on…` attributes are stripped from rendered HTML | Handlers bound by the framework (`onClick`, `@click`, `(click)`) |\n| **Lifecycle** | DOM events (`reef:render`, `reef:signal`) | A framework-specific lifecycle API |\n| **Size** | ~2 KB compressed | From a few tens to several hundred KB, depending on the framework and the app |\n\nWhat Reef does not do — routing, form handling, UI components — is left to the application or to a\nlibrary. That is where Nodus fits: components that are also functions returning strings, and that\ndrop straight into a Reef template.\n\n## What this means with Nodus\n\n- **Event delegation**: one listener per event type, reading `data-action`\n  (see [Conventions and Reef pitfalls](../../../GUIDELINES.md), in French).\n- **Pure templates**: same data, same HTML — never a random id in a template.\n- **Interactive components** (`reef-ignore`): Reef leaves their content alone; a *behavior* takes\n  care of it. To make such a component follow the state, see controlled mode and\n  `registry.remount()`.\n\nTo begin: [Getting started](#/guides/getting-started).\n"